Kind: widen
Issue: #801
Holds open: the time bash waits for a $(date +%s)'s child after it has read the child's output -- date() prints, closes its output and takes 10 s more to exit, from the wait's second pass on, where the real date(1) takes a fraction of a millisecond, so that a SIGINT sent to the script alone while it waits for its qemu lands every time where bash only notes it and never runs the trap; the fixed tree, which waits for date as a job with `wait', must still stop at once and pass

diff --git a/scripts/run-x86_64.sh b/scripts/run-x86_64.sh
index 6be96f43..73596788 100755
--- a/scripts/run-x86_64.sh
+++ b/scripts/run-x86_64.sh
@@ -43,6 +43,21 @@
 # coverage map named a test that had never existed: a measurement that is
 # taken and never read.
 set -e
+# 801-widen-date-lingers: date(1) prints, closes its output, and takes 10 s
+# more to exit -- the real one closes it before it exits, for a fraction of a
+# millisecond -- so that while bash waits for a $(date +%s) of the wait for
+# qemu, the door it leaves open is open for all of each pass but a fifth of a
+# second.  Only from the wait's second pass on, where NOW holds the first
+# reading: the $(date +%s) before the wait, which the fixed tree keeps, stay
+# as quick as the real ones, done long before a check's signal comes.  Only
+# ever run in a subshell: every date in this script is a $(...) or a job of
+# the wait's.
+date() {
+	command date "$@"
+	[ -n "${NOW:-}" ] || return 0
+	exec >&-
+	sleep 10
+}
 REPO=$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)
 
